# Target C-II — collapsed support-six branch forces positive stress slack

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** conditional proved candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the collapsed degree-pure support-six one-anchor branch  
**Dependencies:** exact support-six incidence `K_6\setminus M`; audited-working train-closure host equations (§151); local no-parallel-side theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_support6_collapsed_host_anchor_deficiency_2026_09_18.py`

This note extracts a global consequence that is not visible in the six local closure
equations. It does not eliminate the positive-slack collapsed branch, but it rules out
the branch in the exact `s_Z=0` sector and forces the smallest remaining case to have
one-dimensional normalized KKT stress freedom.

The dependency chain is

```text
collapsed support-six incidence
  -> connected host graph K6 minus a perfect matching
  -> one common outer-anchor projective direction
  -> one-dimensional transpose host kernel
  -> sigma_C = 1
  -> s_Z >= 1 by the audited mixed-rigidity identity.
```

## Exact host-kernel calculation

Use the audited host form of the face-closure transpose equations. For bounded host
vectors `y_F`, a bounded-bounded train `FG` with projective direction `u_FG` gives

\[
 (y_F-y_G)\cdot J u_{FG}=0,
\tag{1}
\]

and a bounded-to-outer train with direction `u_F` gives

\[
 y_F\cdot J u_F=0.
\tag{2}
\]

In the collapsed branch all six outer directions are projectively parallel. Choose
their common direction as `e_1`. Equation (2) then forces

\[
 y_F=t_F e_1
\]

for every one of the six hosts. At each present internal edge, the physical
no-parallel-side theorem says `u_FG` is not parallel to the anchor direction. Hence
`e_1\cdot J u_FG\ne0`, and (1) gives `t_F=t_G`.

The internal host graph is

\[
R=K_6\setminus\{03,14,25\},
\]

which is connected. Therefore all `t_F` are equal and

\[
\boxed{\dim\ker C_{face}^{T}=1.}
\tag{3}
\]

This is an exact incidence-and-direction argument; it does not use the numerical
local realization and does not assume that the auxiliary genus-two side pairing is
the physical source.

## Consequence for canonical KKT slack

The audited train-closure identity gives

\[
\sigma_C=\dim\ker C_{face}^{T},
\]

and the canonical mixed-rigidity/KKT inclusion gives

\[
\sigma_C\le s_Z.
\]

Thus every global canonical representative containing this collapsed support-six
host component satisfies

\[
\boxed{s_Z\ge1.}
\tag{4}
\]

In particular, the collapsed branch is impossible in the exact zero-slack sector
`s_Z=0`. If `s_Z=1`, the normalized KKT stress polytope has exactly one affine
degree of freedom at the global level, subject to the other active components and
the full source constraints. The local two-parameter angle family is not being
identified with that KKT degree; the two objects live in different spaces.

## Scope and next obstruction

The result is conditional on the audited-working §151 host-equation and
mixed-rigidity identities; it does not promote those modules or change the
canonical theorem-state ledger. It does not exclude the positive-slack collapsed
branch. A complete closure now needs either:

1. a theorem incompatible with a collapsed one-anchor component and `s_Z\ge1`;
2. an endpoint/KKT-support argument at `s_Z=1`; or
3. a source-preserving deformation or exact triangle using the global train and
   diameter-bar geometry.

The certified local realization remains a binding regression against purely local
exclusions.

**Disposition:** conditional proved candidate; collapsed `s_Z=0` excluded, positive
slack collapsed branch remains open, C-II remains open.
